  },
  {
   "id": "family_of_struggling.explain.002",
   "kind": "explain",
   "stage": ["explore"],
   "text": {
    "en": "Some approaches teach families how to communicate in a way that makes it more likely that a loved one will accept help, and how to stay safe. A counsellor or a family support group can introduce these."
   },
   "ageBand": ["adult"],
   "tags": ["family-of", "craft", "family-support"],
   "triggers": ["how can i help", "how do i talk to him"],
   "doNotSay": [],
   "sources": [
    {"type": "background", "section": "RES_H1 3.7", "note": "Family-based approaches that help a loved one engage with treatment are described in the brief as general knowledge; clinician to supply a source."}
   ],
   "evidenceLevel": "C",
   "risk": "low",
   "askPermission": true,
   "needsScholarReview": false,
   "needsClinicianReview": true,
   "needsNativeClinicianReview": false,
   "reviewedBy": null,
   "reviewedOn": null,
   "status": "draft"
  },
  {
   "id": "family_of_struggling.explain.003",
   "kind": "explain",
   "stage": ["explore"],
   "text": {
    "en": "Shouting, threats or pleading rarely persuade someone in the middle of using. A calm moment, short and kind words, and a clear offer of help tend to work better."
   },
   "ageBand": ["adult"],
   "tags": ["family-of", "communication"],
   "triggers": ["i shouted", "i begged", "how to talk"],
   "doNotSay": [],
   "sources": [
    {"type": "background", "section": "RES_H1 3.7", "note": "Family-based approaches that help a loved one engage with treatment are described in the brief as general knowledge; clinician to supply a source."}
   ],
   "evidenceLevel": "C",
   "risk": "low",
   "askPermission": true,
   "needsScholarReview": false,
   "needsClinicianReview": true,
   "needsNativeClinicianReview": false,
   "reviewedBy": null,
   "reviewedOn": null,
   "status": "draft"
  },
  {
   "id": "family_of_struggling.explain.004",
   "kind": "explain",
   "stage": ["explore"],
   "text": {
    "en": "Paying their debts, making excuses for them or covering up can feel like love and still keep the problem going. A family worker can help you work out what to keep doing and what to stop."
   },
   "ageBand": ["adult"],
   "tags": ["family-of", "enabling"],
   "triggers": ["i paid his debts", "i lied for him"],
   "doNotSay": ["Never \"tough love\" or \"cut him off\"."],
   "sources": [
    {"type": "background", "section": "RES_H1 3.7", "note": "Family-based approaches that help a loved one engage with treatment are described in the brief as general knowledge; clinician to supply a source."}
